# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the `native-tsan` job (N2-29; docs/native/rhi.md, "The ThreadSanitizer job").
#
# Used as TSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1:suppressions=<repo>/tools/native/tsan.supp. The Vulkan loader and the software
# driver the job runs on (lavapipe, from mesa-vulkan-drivers, with the LLVM library its JIT compiler is) are not built
# with ThreadSanitizer, so it cannot see the synchronization inside them (their own locks, futexes and thread pool) and
# could report accesses that are in fact ordered, with stacks that end in the library.
#
# On the machine N2-29 was written on (loader 1.3.275, Mesa 25.2.8) these entries never fire: the tests report nothing
# with an empty file too. They are there for a driver build that differs, and they name only those libraries. Never add
# a frame of plume (native/third_party/rhi/plume/patched) or of our code here: a report that has one is a race in the
# code under test (the control D2 of native/tests/rhi_plume_patterns_test.cpp makes one on purpose and is not run by the
# job), and the fix is the code.
#
# They are `race:` and `mutex:` entries, not `called_from_lib:`: a called_from_lib entry for any of these libraries made
# ThreadSanitizer worse here (it stops the process with "library ... is unloaded" when the loader dlcloses the driver,
# which every instance destruction does, and with an entry for the loader or the JIT library it reported "unlock of an
# unlocked mutex" and data races in operator delete in code that is clean without it).

# the Vulkan loader, lavapipe's driver library and the LLVM library its JIT compiler is, as a frame of a report
race:libvulkan.so
race:libvulkan_lvp.so
race:libLLVM.so

# the loader's own mutexes
mutex:libvulkan.so
